A||N||10||Sndfr||Thoughts on V8 conversion from 4 cyl?||||5||
Z||000000||Sndfr||11-23-2000||02:00 PM||||I'm brand new to the Fiero world and lovin' it.  The thing that interests me the most is V8 conversions.  Especially from a "starting with a 4 cylinder" point of view.  I'm just askin' for everyone's two cents on that subject.<P>Love it, hate it, either way...<P>------------------<BR>-matthew d.<BR>"who's yo daddy?"<P>'84 SE||||reg||5
Z||000001||Philphine||11-23-2000||02:18 PM||||i want to do a swap of some kind. it may just be a v6, but i'm studying all the options and hoping for a chance to get rid of my 4cyl.<P>welcome anyway. throw your 2 cents in and soon we'll have a jar fulla knowledge.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe (cheap thrill)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||11-23-2000||04:33 PM||||Be forewarned just about any V6 of V8 swap into a 4 banger is going to run you multi thousands of dollars.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Zorba||11-23-2000||09:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>Be forewarned just about any V6 of V8 swap into a 4 banger is going to run you multi thousands of dollars.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I just did one. Plan on $4,000-$10,000 depending on which motor you use and the parts you buy. it's not brain surgery but you have to be careful, follow instructions<BR>and use an ARCHIE conversion kit for starters.  But if you have $10,000 to spare you can always buy one that's already converted between $7,000-$10,000. But if you are going the v8 route, call Archie.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||11-24-2000||03:49 PM||||Well how much is it compared to converting a v6 to a v8? If it doesn't cost that much more than a 4cyl would be fine to start with.<P>What about a 4 to a non 2.8 based v6? <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tryxalon||11-24-2000||09:19 PM||||I'm planning a swap from a 4 cylinder to a 92 3100. I removed the 3100 from my niece's  wrecked 92 Olds, I have all the wiring from the fusebox through the ECM and the Engine. I pulled engine with transmission and both axles, Torque struts, motormounts.  --- everything I thought I might need, and a few other things to "MacGuyver with". <P>I am looking at spending $1,000 dollars MORE at a minimum, and expecting closer to $2,000 in "twiddles, thingys, natters and bobs" to make it work. <P>IF you have to buy an engine and neccesary parts - even used parts ... expect much more. <P>A Chevy Crate V-8 Engine $3200 and V-8 Archie's Master Kit (about $5000-$6000 and keeping your transmission) will run you about $10,000. Expect more if someone does it for you. <P>Engine swaps are never "cheap". <P>  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Archie||11-24-2000||09:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tryxalon:<BR><B><BR>A Chevy Crate V-8 Engine $3200 and V-8 Archie's Master Kit (about $5000-$6000 and keeping your transmission) will run you about $10,000. Expect more if someone does it for you. <P>Engine swaps are never "cheap". <P>  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Your right Engine swaps are not always cheap, however you have inflated the cost of the V-8 swap a little.<P>The Master-Build Kit is $3150<BR>A new SBC LM1 long block = $ 1350<BR>That engine will need a carb, ignition system and intake manifold = appox. $450<P>So if you do your own labor and pay retail prices for everything, you can do a very nice V-8 swap for $ 4950, & THAT'S with a NEW engine.<P>Besides that when you compare engine swap costs you should compare apples to apples. Comparing  a used 1992 3.1 swap to a NEW 5.7 SBC swap is not a fair comparison. <P>I would submit to you that a 3.1 swap that uses a used engine and costs $2000 or more, is a lesser R.O.I. than a 5.7 swap with a new engine for &lt;$5000.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000007||Zorba||11-25-2000||01:34 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B> Your right Engine swaps are not always cheap,   </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Get your visa out, buy archie's basic kit, buy a newly rebuilt engine $800 for a 325HP motor, $1,000 maybe. or $1,500 for a NEW one.<BR>And that would get you started for under $2,500 plus peripherals, $500.00 say under $3,000. Now you have your v8 conversion. You can add the other stuff later, 4-core radiator, water pump, (archi's drive or electric) starter , etc. If you get a engine for less than $800 than it's even better, if you know it runs right. So this $10,000 deal is inflated to make the 3.1 swap look cheaper. If you want more power, go with a v8 set up. I think anything else is non-sense and a waste of time. Might as well turbo charge your i-4 or v-6. Although a v8 is not going to give the gas mileage, neither would a new dodge viper. You pay for power!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||PlasticWrapd||11-27-2000||10:01 PM||||Ack...  I had the chance to drive a Fiero with a 350 TPI engine.  Awesome acceleration, the seats should have more padding  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  My only problem is the car becomes a straight line racer.  The added weight has a marked effect on the handling, imho.  Cornering was poor, even with the larger sway bars and stiffer springs.  Felt like the car was plowing into the turns as compared to my 4-banger.  I'd go for the 3800 or the NorthStar swap ( only 30 lbs heavier than the 2.8).  Just my $.02||||reg||13
Z||000009||Tryxalon||11-28-2000||08:35 PM||||Thanks for the correction Archie, I freely admit inflating the price - Based on an estimate I made for swaping to a V-8. <P>I used a price of $3200 for the crate engine because that is what the most expensive "complete w/carb and pan" 350 SBC engine estimate from my local Chevy dealer.<BR> Yes, I added about 1-2000 dollars to the cost of your kit because every engine swap I've ever done, I've also replaced all the "wearables" and the "might as wells" (shocks, brakes, hoses, cables and all) and wound up paying much more than I intended.  <P>Yeah, I was looking at maximum $ because of all the times I've been bit by <BR>"Cheops Corolary To Murphy's Law:  Nothing ever gets done on time, or within budget."<BR> I hate going back to "do it right the second time". <P>And I would agree with you on the ROI EXCEPT I know how that 3100 V-6 was treated for all the miles on it and I got it for the total price of $0.00. (ZERO) <P>On the other hand, I mentioned what I expected to spend on my "on the cheap" swap not as an endorcement of a 3100 swap, but as a minimum cost of a swap if EVERYTHING is done "by the lowest bidder". (Like a government project)<P>That being said - Archie knows his kit and the associated costs better than my admitted inflated estimates. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 11-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||11-29-2000||10:02 AM||||Tryx,<BR>Please let us know how it goes. Several members have expressed interest in swapping in the aluminum head V-6's (3.1s and 3.4s). Most people haven't, because it does require the swap of the ECM to support the DIS. Always thought the SFI 3.4 would really wake up a Fiero. <P>Raydar||||reg||1
